1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,800
On July 8, 1947, Fort Worth Star-Telegram photographer J. Bond Johnson caught

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:10,860
General Roger Ramey holding a typed message, probably a teletype, at the press

3
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:13,670
session where Roswell's flying disc became a weather balloon.

4
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:17,630
For almost forty years, researchers have tried to read that slip of paper.

5
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:22,900
Now it has been through the latest in publicly available AI:

6
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:26,485
Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5, and Jev, from TypeSafe.

7
00:00:26,935 --> 00:00:32,875
Opus 5.5 rebuilt the image analysis from the raw scans. Jev, a new kind of

8
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:36,745
model that answers narrow questions with probabilities, judged the words.

9
00:00:37,195 --> 00:00:39,685
The question is simple: can AI read it?

10
00:00:40,685 --> 00:00:42,185
Each system had a different job.

11
00:00:42,635 --> 00:00:48,135
Claude Opus 5.5 wrote and ran every step of the image analysis: aligning

12
00:00:48,135 --> 00:00:52,735
the scans, measuring the film grain, straightening the typed lines, and

13
00:00:52,735 --> 00:00:56,215
testing whether letter matching can find words nobody disputes.

14
00:00:56,665 --> 00:01:02,305
Jev, version 1.13, cannot see images. It reads text and returns a probability

15
00:01:02,305 --> 00:01:05,185
for each question, so it got the questions about words.

16
00:01:05,635 --> 00:01:10,535
When ten researchers transcribe the same spot, do two readings name the same word,

17
00:01:10,535 --> 00:01:15,495
like DISC spelled with a C or with a K, or different words, like VICTIMS and

18
00:01:15,495 --> 00:01:15,865
VIEWING?

19
00:01:16,315 --> 00:01:19,974
And is each factual sentence in this script supported by the measurements?

20
00:01:20,424 --> 00:01:24,884
In every case, code, not the models, sets the thresholds and keeps the score.

21
00:01:25,884 --> 00:01:31,644
UTA posts twenty-three scan files. Claude Opus 5.5 worked from nine of them.

22
00:01:32,094 --> 00:01:36,474
Left and right halves of the memo, labelled eight hundred dots per inch, at

23
00:01:36,474 --> 00:01:40,464
four brightness levels each, plus one whole-memo file labelled a thousand.

24
00:01:40,914 --> 00:01:45,674
The film is a negative, so the typed letters show up light. Before reading

25
00:01:45,674 --> 00:01:49,056
anything, Opus 5.5 measured what the files actually contain.

26
00:01:50,056 --> 00:01:53,116
On the right half, the four brightness files line up to a sixth of

27
00:01:53,116 --> 00:01:56,386
a pixel, and their grain correlates at point eight to point nine.

28
00:01:56,836 --> 00:01:59,896
On the left half, two of the files had shifted against the others by

29
00:01:59,896 --> 00:02:03,786
up to thirty-five pixels, and the shift bends across the frame.

30
00:02:04,236 --> 00:02:09,016
Opus 5.5 mapped that shift point by point and undid it. The

31
00:02:09,016 --> 00:02:11,836
grain of the two shifted files then matched at almost point

32
00:02:11,836 --> 00:02:15,596
nine: one negative, with the same grain in every file.

33
00:02:16,046 --> 00:02:19,026
So merging all four files removes only five to eight

34
00:02:19,026 --> 00:02:22,056
percent of the noise. It adds no new letters.

35
00:02:23,056 --> 00:02:24,686
Here are the numbers that govern everything.

36
00:02:25,136 --> 00:02:29,976
The file names say 2x. The pixels say more: a tell-tale pattern repeats

37
00:02:29,976 --> 00:02:34,476
every eleven pixels, and the real detail stops at about one sample every

38
00:02:34,476 --> 00:02:38,746
three and a half file pixels. The files were enlarged in software.

39
00:02:39,196 --> 00:02:41,156
A grain clump is about sixteen pixels across. A

40
00:02:41,156 --> 00:02:44,573
typed letter is about two hundred and twenty.

41
00:02:45,023 --> 00:02:48,783
And the letters are soft. Fitting the known words, the best match came

42
00:02:48,783 --> 00:02:53,153
with a blur about forty-seven pixels wide, a fifth of a letter.

43
00:02:53,603 --> 00:02:55,883
That smears the strokes that tell an E from

44
00:02:55,883 --> 00:02:58,563
an F. Then the grain covers what is left.

45
00:02:59,563 --> 00:03:04,563
Opus 5.5 tried Gaussian smoothing, total-variation denoising, non-local means,

46
00:03:04,563 --> 00:03:08,712
median filtering, and smoothing at the size of the measured blur.

47
00:03:09,162 --> 00:03:12,472
Every method produced the same result: words as blobs.

48
00:03:12,922 --> 00:03:16,520
Light smoothing leaves the grain. Heavy smoothing takes the strokes with it.

49
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:19,276
What could be fixed was the geometry.

50
00:03:19,726 --> 00:03:24,846
The paper is curled and tilted. Across a half-scan, one typed line drifts up or down

51
00:03:24,846 --> 00:03:30,156
by as much as three hundred and forty pixels, more than half the gap between lines.

52
00:03:30,606 --> 00:03:34,626
Opus 5.5 traced each line by following its pattern of letters

53
00:03:34,626 --> 00:03:38,536
from column to column, and resampled every line flat.

54
00:03:38,986 --> 00:03:41,646
Then it joined the two halves where they overlap, about twenty-one

55
00:03:41,646 --> 00:03:46,046
hundred pixels. In six of six patches the grain matches at point

56
00:03:46,046 --> 00:03:49,528
nine two or better: both halves are the same piece of film.

57
00:03:50,528 --> 00:03:54,008
This is the cleanest view of the memo that Opus 5.5 could get

58
00:03:54,008 --> 00:03:58,278
from these scans: nine lines, straightened and joined.

59
00:03:58,728 --> 00:04:01,668
On the right, where three lines crowd together, the tracer could

60
00:04:01,668 --> 00:04:04,531
not keep them apart, and those stretches are left blank.

61
00:04:04,981 --> 00:04:06,873
Word lengths and spacing are clear.

62
00:04:07,323 --> 00:04:10,083
A seven-letter word before OF THE WRECK on line two.

63
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:12,526
The short line: AT FORT WORTH, TEX.

64
00:04:12,976 --> 00:04:16,266
WEATHER BALLOONS on line seven, and a short signature on line nine.

65
00:04:16,716 --> 00:04:18,266
Letters, though, are still blobs.

66
00:04:19,266 --> 00:04:21,093
So what does the memo probably say?

67
00:04:21,543 --> 00:04:24,483
David Rudiak has compiled ten readers' transcriptions,

68
00:04:24,483 --> 00:04:28,173
side by side, across seventy-four word positions.

69
00:04:28,623 --> 00:04:32,983
Jev compared every pair of readings at every position, five hundred and

70
00:04:32,983 --> 00:04:36,783
eleven pairs, and judged whether each pair names the same word.

71
00:04:37,233 --> 00:04:40,293
Only five word positions are unanimous: OF and THE on

72
00:04:40,293 --> 00:04:43,836
line two, and AT, FORT and WORTH on line three.

73
00:04:44,286 --> 00:04:47,046
Fourteen reach eight of ten readers. Twenty-two

74
00:04:47,046 --> 00:04:49,526
reach six, under thirty percent of the memo.

75
00:04:49,976 --> 00:04:53,896
Jev merged the C and K spellings of DISC, so seven readers see

76
00:04:53,896 --> 00:04:58,256
a quoted disc. RAMEY has five readers and TEMPLE three, so at

77
00:04:58,256 --> 00:05:00,726
a six-reader threshold, the signature does not match.

78
00:05:01,176 --> 00:05:04,136
Moving Jev's same-word threshold anywhere from point three

79
00:05:04,136 --> 00:05:08,202
to point nine changes these counts by at most one.

80
00:05:08,652 --> 00:05:13,812
What survives is a skeleton: a wreck, something forwarded to Fort Worth,

81
00:05:13,812 --> 00:05:17,732
and weather balloons. Whether the message was a correction or a cover story

82
00:05:17,732 --> 00:05:21,152
is exactly what the unclear words would decide, and they don't.

83
00:05:22,152 --> 00:05:26,532
The fight is over one word. Seven letters, before OF THE WRECK.

84
00:05:26,982 --> 00:05:31,442
VICTIMS, say six of the ten readers, among them David Rudiak,

85
00:05:31,442 --> 00:05:34,342
who has worked on this memo for more than twenty-five years.

86
00:05:34,792 --> 00:05:40,564
REMAINS, say two others. FINDING, says Brad Sparks. VIEWING has been suggested too.

87
00:05:41,014 --> 00:05:45,054
By Jev's count, VICTIMS just clears the six-reader bar. But

88
00:05:45,054 --> 00:05:48,081
agreement among readers is not evidence from the pixels.

89
00:05:48,531 --> 00:05:53,491
In 2016, Rudiak reported an unpublished test: letter templates

90
00:05:53,491 --> 00:05:57,651
scored VICTIMS above its rivals and two nonsense words, and

91
00:05:57,651 --> 00:05:59,911
about as well as FORT WORTH and WEATHER BALLOONS.

92
00:06:00,361 --> 00:06:04,481
Opus 5.5 asked the question that has to come first:

93
00:06:04,481 --> 00:06:07,377
can the method find words that nobody disputes?

94
00:06:08,377 --> 00:06:09,197
Here is the test.

95
00:06:09,647 --> 00:06:16,347
Opus 5.5 rendered five undisputed phrases in four monospace fonts, over a grid of

96
00:06:16,347 --> 00:06:20,807
letter sizes, spacings and blurs, and slid each one along its straightened line.

97
00:06:21,257 --> 00:06:24,017
The letter size and blur were fitted on the other four phrases,

98
00:06:24,017 --> 00:06:27,097
so no phrase was tested on settings tuned to itself.

99
00:06:27,547 --> 00:06:30,667
Then it scored every one-letter misspelling of each phrase, and

100
00:06:30,667 --> 00:06:34,667
three hundred strings of random letters with the same spacing.

101
00:06:35,117 --> 00:06:41,737
FORT WORTH, TEX. came a hundred and eighty-third out of six hundred and one. A

102
00:06:41,737 --> 00:06:44,517
hundred and thirty-two misspellings beat it, and so did fifty random strings.

103
00:06:44,967 --> 00:06:49,547
WEATHER BALLOONS came a hundred and fifty-third. OF THE WRECK, a hundred and

104
00:06:49,547 --> 00:06:53,907
forty-ninth. RAMEY lost to two hundred and nineteen of the three hundred random

105
00:06:53,907 --> 00:06:54,427
strings.

106
00:06:54,877 --> 00:06:59,798
Some random strings beat every true phrase: eight of three hundred for WEATHER

107
00:06:59,798 --> 00:07:05,438
BALLOONS, and up to two hundred and nineteen for RAMEY. The method cannot reliably

108
00:07:05,438 --> 00:07:08,867
pick real words from random letters, let alone one letter from another.

109
00:07:09,867 --> 00:07:14,128
Why does it fail? Opus 5.5 built fake memo lines where the answer is

110
00:07:14,128 --> 00:07:18,907
known: a true phrase, typeset, laid on real blank film from the same

111
00:07:18,907 --> 00:07:22,077
negative, at the letter strength measured on the real lines.

112
00:07:22,527 --> 00:07:26,147
Five phrases on four patches of film make twenty trials, and

113
00:07:26,147 --> 00:07:29,127
the search included the right font at the right size.

114
00:07:29,577 --> 00:07:31,798
With sharp letters, the true phrase usually made the

115
00:07:31,798 --> 00:07:34,897
top ten, and came first in four of twenty trials.

116
00:07:35,347 --> 00:07:38,257
Blurred the way the memo's letters are, it came first in none of twenty.

117
00:07:38,707 --> 00:07:40,707
Take the grain away, and even the blurred phrase

118
00:07:40,707 --> 00:07:42,817
comes first, five times out of five.

119
00:07:43,267 --> 00:07:46,307
Turn the letters up: sharp letters start winning at one and a half

120
00:07:46,307 --> 00:07:49,928
times the real strength, blurred letters only at six times.

121
00:07:50,377 --> 00:07:53,377
It takes the blur and the grain together to defeat the method,

122
00:07:53,377 --> 00:07:57,638
and the memo has both. Its own scores suggest its letters are,

123
00:07:57,638 --> 00:08:00,527
if anything, a little weaker than these tests assumed.

124
00:08:01,527 --> 00:08:03,077
And the disputed word itself?

125
00:08:03,527 --> 00:08:08,667
The method could not even find where the known words sit: its best positions put OF

126
00:08:08,667 --> 00:08:13,927
THE WRECK and FORWARDED five letters apart, when the memo has seventeen between

127
00:08:13,927 --> 00:08:18,264
them. So Opus 5.5 searched for the disputed word along the whole of line two.

128
00:08:18,714 --> 00:08:23,274
FINDING came first out of three hundred and seven. REMAINS came fourth.

129
00:08:23,274 --> 00:08:26,764
VICTIMS came thirty-first, behind twenty-six random strings.

130
00:08:27,214 --> 00:08:30,254
Because the method fails on words nobody disputes,

131
00:08:30,254 --> 00:08:32,854
this settles nothing, for or against VICTIMS.

132
00:08:33,304 --> 00:08:35,624
Any letter ranking on this word, from anyone, should

133
00:08:35,624 --> 00:08:37,854
be asked to pass the known-word test first.

134
00:08:38,854 --> 00:08:42,924
This matters because of a 2002 experiment by James Houran and Kevin Randle.

135
00:08:43,374 --> 00:08:47,394
A hundred and seventy-six volunteers tried to read the memo.

136
00:08:47,394 --> 00:08:51,094
One group was told it was about Roswell, one about atomic-bomb

137
00:08:51,094 --> 00:08:53,020
testing, and one only that it was a document.

138
00:08:53,470 --> 00:08:56,530
The Roswell group found crash words, the atomic group found

139
00:08:56,530 --> 00:08:59,540
atomic words, and the third group read very little.

140
00:08:59,990 --> 00:09:04,551
Yet FORT WORTH, STORY, and WEATHER BALLOONS turned up in every group.

141
00:09:05,001 --> 00:09:08,541
The pixels underdetermine the words, and context fills the gap.

142
00:09:09,541 --> 00:09:11,752
So, can AI read the Ramey memo?

143
00:09:12,202 --> 00:09:15,722
No. Not even the latest AI, not from these scans,

144
00:09:15,722 --> 00:09:17,362
and not by matching letter templates.

145
00:09:17,812 --> 00:09:23,332
What Claude Opus 5.5 added: evidence that every file carries one negative's

146
00:09:23,332 --> 00:09:27,772
grain and was enlarged in software, a straightened view of all nine lines,

147
00:09:27,772 --> 00:09:32,152
and a known-answer test showing that the memo's blur and grain together

148
00:09:32,152 --> 00:09:34,822
defeat template matching, even with the right font.

149
00:09:35,272 --> 00:09:40,352
What Jev added: a word-by-word count of where ten human readers truly agree, and

150
00:09:40,352 --> 00:09:44,022
a check of every factual sentence in this script against the measurements.

151
00:09:44,472 --> 00:09:47,432
One of its flags sent Opus 5.5 back to the sharp-letter

152
00:09:47,432 --> 00:09:50,932
test, and fixing that test changed a conclusion.

153
00:09:51,382 --> 00:09:56,212
The word skeleton survives. VICTIMS remains a reading, not a fact.

154
00:09:56,662 --> 00:10:00,162
And the ten-thousand-dollar reward, offered in 2016 for

155
00:10:00,162 --> 00:10:03,682
a definitive reading, has never been publicly claimed.

156
00:10:04,682 --> 00:10:07,242
The methods, the test results, and Jev's agreement

157
00:10:07,242 --> 00:10:09,108
table are summarized in the description.

158
00:10:09,558 --> 00:10:11,036
This has been The Future Past.
